    "response": "It is understandable to question the fairness of the process. An all-male panel in senior banking is a significant contextual factor, but whether it is actionable depends on distinguishing between **cultural indicators of bias** and **formal evidence of discrimination**.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Is it a sign of anything?\n\nYes, it is often a sign of several things:\n\n* **Affinity and In-Group Bias:** Decades of organizational research indicate that homogeneous interview panels are prone to unconscious affinity bias—gravitating toward candidates who look, speak, and have backgrounds similar to their own.\n* **Corporate Culture & Representation:** An all-male panel for a senior appointment demonstrates that either the bank lacks women in senior risk and leadership roles, or it does not prioritize balanced panels in its hiring governance. Many modern institutions mandate at least one woman or independent panelist on executive interview loops precisely to mitigate this issue.\n* **Shifting Evaluation Criteria:** In quantitative fields, women often face a double standard where deep technical credentials (e.g., your PhD in statistics and 9 years in risk modelling) are recast as \"too specialized,\" while a male candidate’s broader background (e.g., \"regulatory affairs\" and an MSc in finance) is framed as \"strategic leadership.\" \n\n---\n\n### 2. Should you make something of it?\n\nHow you approach this depends on your objective—whether you are seeking closure/learning, considering a formal challenge, or deciding how to view this organization.\n\n#### A. As a Legal / Formal Matter (Caution Advised)\n* **By itself, an all-male panel is not proof of unlawful discrimination.** Employers are legally entitled to select a candidate they believe has skills better aligned with their current priorities (in this case, they appear to have prioritized regulatory liaison experience over technical quantitative depth).\n* To establish sex discrimination, there usually needs to be evidence of procedural unfairness—for example:\n  * Inconsistent interview questions or scoring criteria.\n  * Shifting role specifications between the original job description and the final selection rationale.\n  * Direct comments reflecting gender or age bias during the interview process.\n\n#### B. As an Informational Signal (Very Meaningful)\n* It tells you a great deal about the environment you would have been entering. If leadership recruitment is not held to basic diversity standards, you may have faced continuous friction in getting technical initiatives supported or obtaining sponsorship internally.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Send a Polite Follow-Up for Feedback:**\n   * Two weeks is a reasonable window after which to send a polite reminder.\n   * Frame the request around professional development rather than suspicion:\n     > *\"I wanted to follow up on my request for feedback regarding the Head of Risk Modelling role. As I was among the final candidates, understanding where the panel felt my profile had gaps—particularly relative to the strategic and regulatory focus of the department—would be immensely valuable for my ongoing development.\"*\n2. **Review the Original Job Specification:**\n   * Compare the advertised requirements against the announcement. Did the original spec list a PhD/advanced quantitative degree or risk modelling expertise as essential, with regulatory affairs as secondary? If they hired someone who did not meet advertised *minimum* or *essential* requirements that you exceeded, that is a much stronger indicator of a compromised process.\n3. **Keep Detailed Records:**\n   * Archive the job posting, your notes from the two interview rounds, the names of the panel members, the hiring announcement, and all correspondence.\n4. **Consult an Employment Specialist (if you suspect bad faith):**\n   * If you are an internal candidate or if the bank operates in a jurisdiction with strong equality legislation (such as the UK Equality Act 2010 or US Title VII/EEOC), and the feedback is either evasive or indicates inconsistent criteria, you can consult an employment lawyer or union/advisory body (e.g., ACAS in the UK) for an initial assessment before escalating.\n",
